cConkey</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 ARI surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone ARI throw it (59% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Michael Wilson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Script leans LAC.</strong> As a 12-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Omarion Hampton</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile ARI throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Michael Wilson</strong>.</p><p>Fade the ARI run \u2014 as 12-point dogs projected to chase, <strong>James Conner</strong>'s carries get squeezed, though his receiving work ticks up in catch-up mode.</p><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>LAC \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>LAC are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 12-point favorite projected to lead for 56% of snaps, <strong>LAC</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Omarion Hampton</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>10 carries for 43 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as LAC salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Ladd McConkey</strong> (5 targets, 42 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2